<Base
  type="article"
  title="Most teams do not have an AI adoption problem"
  description="They have a decision-design problem. If no one can say which judgment should improve, another tool will only make the old process move faster."
>
  <WritingSample slug="ai-is-a-decision-design-problem" pattern="Tension → Mechanism → Implication">
    <p>
      If no one can say which judgment should improve, who owns it and what
      evidence would change their mind, another tool will only make the old
      process move faster.
    </p>

    <p>
      Most rollouts stall in the same place. A team names an AI capability,
      picks a tool and a pilot team, and then discovers the real problem was
      never adoption. The decision the tool was supposed to sharpen was never
      defined. So the pilot returns better inputs into a process that still
      cannot say which decision changed as a result.
    </p>

    <p>
      The corrective is upstream of the tool. Before AI enters a workflow,
      three questions have to have real answers, written down:
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Which judgment is this meant to improve?</strong> Not
      “efficiency” or “quality” — the specific call someone has to make.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Who owns that judgment?</strong> If ownership is diffuse, no
      one benefits from a better input and no one is accountable for a worse
      one.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>What evidence would move it?</strong> If the answer is “more
      confidence”, the judgment is not yet real. Real judgments have failure
      modes that a specific piece of evidence could change.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      Start with the decision. Then decide whether AI belongs in the system.
    </blockquote>
  </WritingSample>
</Base>
